     Above the fathers rather than below, so the page offers a person
     before it offers another shelf of works.

     It takes the dark ground and the fathers take the tan, Ian having
     swapped the two: "make the authors interested background the dark
     gray and the church fathers background tan." The .blist--dark
     modifier moved with it, since that is what lifts the rules and the
     meta lines off a dark ground; left behind it would have printed
     pale grey on tan there and near-black on charcoal here.

     EVERY NAME IS THE CATALOGUE'S OWN. The names as Ian wrote them are
     how these authors are spoken of; they are not how the corpus
     records them, and ?a= is matched against the corpus. So each was
     resolved against v1/graph/authors.json, the 2,608-name index the
     author rooms themselves resolve against, and the most-cited match
     taken where a name is shared: Augustine of Hippo rather than
     Augustine of Canterbury, Ambrose of Milan rather than Ambrose
     Autpert. All thirty were then opened and checked to render a real
     room, including the three thinnest, Basil, Turretin and Owen.

     THE BOLD NAME IS IAN'S, the line under it is the catalogue's. That
     way the column reads alphabetically as he listed it, Aquinas under
     A rather than Thomas Aquinas under T, while the link and the page
     it opens still agree about who this is. Two of his had slipped a
     letter, Turriten and Own; they are Turretin and Owen here. --}}
